WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.766
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.057 --> 00:00:05.390
REENA NADKARNI: Hello, everyone.

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:06.340
Thank you for coming.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:09.720
This is the session on
enterprise security and G Suite

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:10.810
tools.

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:13.960
I'm going Reena Nadkarni,
Product Manager on the G Suite

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:14.950
Team.

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:18.099
We also have with us today,
Ivon Passos, my colleague,

00:00:18.099 --> 00:00:19.765
who's going to show
us some great demos.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:29.450
So let's start by thinking
about why this matters.

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:34.010
Why does enterprise
cloud security matter?

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:37.280
Gartner recently said that
the cloud first strategies

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:40.790
are the foundation
of staying relevant

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:42.690
in this fast-paced world.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:47.420
So as you can see,
cloud is a fundamental,

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.120
once-in-a-generation shift.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:52.340
And you, as leaders
of innovation,

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:54.335
cannot afford to
get left behind.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.230
But we understand that
these shift to the cloud

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.990
come with some
significant risks, risks

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.240
to your enterprise,
to your data,

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.160
as you move this
data to the cloud--

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:11.070
also, to your end users.

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:13.110
So you may be
convinced, but not all

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:15.330
of your end users
may be convinced.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:18.450
And finally, a
personal risk to you.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:22.410
This is your professional
reputation, your job, at stake

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:25.290
if you make the decision
to go to the cloud.

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.420
And I bet none of
you wants to be

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.130
on CNN for the wrong reasons.

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:33.800
I want you to know
that at Google, we

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.710
take this responsibility
very seriously.

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:44.440
Let's first start with
our product principles.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:48.670
At Google, we aim to build
in security at all levels.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:50.710
You saw in the
keynote this morning,

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:54.310
right from our custom hardware
chips, our data centers,

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:57.520
our network infrastructure,
our software,

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.990
as well as end users and
endpoints like mobile.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:06.910
Second, we aim to make our
security easy and natural

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:09.770
to use, not just
for the end users,

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:12.430
but also for the administrators.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:15.460
We want the security
to just work, and not

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:18.730
get in the way of
you doing your work.

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:23.980
And lastly, we want to aim
to help our customers meet

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.280
their compliance needs.

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:32.150
In security, scale matters.

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:36.890
Google has seven properties
with over a billion users each.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:41.660
You're familiar with these,
Gmail, Search, Play, Android,

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:43.300
YouTube, and others--

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:46.400
seven properties with
over a billion users each.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.700
And with G Suite,
the security team

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:51.950
that takes care
of these products

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:53.520
is now working for you.

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:03.170
But we don't just stop there.

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:05.360
We have groups of
security researchers

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:08.630
and former hackers,
who are continuously

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:10.340
monitoring our system.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:13.820
We also can proactively
invite the community

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:15.860
to help us hack our systems.

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:17.950
We run bug bounty
programs, where

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:21.350
we have external hackers come
and try to hack our systems.

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:23.330
But I'm sorry to say,
most of the time,

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:26.630
they get this T-shirt that
says, "I tried to hack Google,

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:29.210
but all I got was
this lousy T-shirt."

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:30.800
You're welcome to
try, by the way.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.940
So that is the solid
foundation of Google security

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.460
that we build our products upon.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:42.970
On top of that, we
at G Suite are always

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:46.690
listening, learning, and
designing to the concerns

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:48.700
of our top customers.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:53.950
In my conversations with our
CIOs, CISOs, IT administrators,

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:57.319
somehow three themes seem
to keep bubbling back up.

00:03:57.319 --> 00:03:59.110
And we are going to
talk about each of them

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:03.610
today, phishing, data breaches,
and mobile management.

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:06.940
Obviously, our customers are
very worried about these.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:09.250
And with this session,
I want to leave you

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:13.330
with a sense of familiarity
and comfort of all the tools

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:14.980
that we have in
each of these areas.

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:19.550
Let's talk about phishing.

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:28.430
Users still fall for a
well-defined phishing page 50%

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:29.630
of the time.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:32.480
Phishing is still
the number one way

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:36.830
someone's going to get malware
into your organizations.

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:40.040
As you know, passwords
can be easily stolen.

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:41.570
And they're weak.

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:45.380
Did you know that two of the
most commonly used passwords

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:52.070
last year were, 123456
and the word, "password"?

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:54.560
So clearly, we have
a problem here.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.140
And OTP codes can
also be phished.

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:59.790
So what do we do?

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:02.970
We recommend that you
use security keys.

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:05.610
Security keys are easy.

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:06.490
They are great.

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:09.550
They help you prevent
against phishing.

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:12.420
What we have also done
is that we have now

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:16.830
added some admin controls to be
able to manage these security

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:18.870
keys at scale.

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:24.090
We now allow you to enforce
the usage of security keys

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:25.500
within your domain.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:29.340
We allow you to let your users
self-enroll these security

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:31.230
keys if you wanted to.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:35.580
If a user lost their security
key, or it got stolen,

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:37.920
you can help that
user recover it.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:42.120
And finally, you can now report
on the usage of security key

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:43.040
within your domain.

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:48.000
I'm going to call Ivon
on stage to show us

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.260
a demo of how this looks.

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:50.960
Ivon?

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:52.476
IVON PASSOS: Thanks, Reena.

00:05:52.476 --> 00:05:53.350
Can you guys hear me?

00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:54.190
Yes.

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:58.400
OK, so can we switch
to the computer?

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:05.270
There we go.

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:09.530
OK, so I'm going to show you
two different views of how

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:10.520
this works.

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:13.700
One is the admin, which
is what you see now.

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:17.240
And you will also see how a
user perceives this feature.

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:21.530
So the first thing is to set
up security keys for a domain--

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:23.270
and you see on
the left hand side

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:26.450
here that I can select this
per OU, or Organization Unit,

00:06:26.450 --> 00:06:27.680
within the domain.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:29.970
You basically have
transferred three questions.

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:33.800
Number one, do you want to
enforce this immediately,

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:35.659
or in a future date?

00:06:35.659 --> 00:06:37.700
And we recommend that you
do it in a future date,

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:40.935
so that you have time to
communicate with your users,

00:06:40.935 --> 00:06:43.310
and let them know they should
be receiving security keys.

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:45.969
They need to self-enroll,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:06:45.969 --> 00:06:47.510
Once you make that
decision, you also

00:06:47.510 --> 00:06:50.090
need to choose whether
you want to allow

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:54.710
all types of two-factor
authentication, which

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:58.700
could be OTP or security
keys, or just security keys.

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:01.910
As Reena mentioned,
OTP is also phishable.

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:03.920
But in some cases,
you may not be

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:07.010
able to deploy security
keys only to everyone.

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:09.987
But if you select
only security key,

00:07:09.987 --> 00:07:11.570
you need to make
another choice, which

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:15.620
is if your user loses or
gets their security stolen,

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:18.050
how much time of a
grace period will you

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:20.990
allow them to use
another method until they

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:22.760
receive the new security key?

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:25.130
And that's set up right here.

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:26.000
That's it.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:28.310
That's all you need
to do to set it up.

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:30.710
Now I'm going to
switch to the user view

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:35.630
to show you how easy the
use of security key is.

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:39.460
So right now, I'm logging
in as a regular user.

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:44.110
And because I set up security
key enforcement on the domain,

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:45.340
I'm going to be prompted.

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:47.710
So would you like to
register your security key?

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:51.190
I have my security key here
that I received from my admin.

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:53.050
And then I can just
click Register.

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:58.652
I'm going to be verified
on my identity again.

00:07:58.652 --> 00:07:59.610
Have your security key?

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:01.185
Yes, next.

00:08:01.185 --> 00:08:04.550
Now I'm going to plug
in the security key.

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:05.780
And I will touch it.

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:09.670
And that's it.

00:08:09.670 --> 00:08:10.960
That's all I need to do.

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:14.980
Once I touch the security
key, I have it registered.

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:17.330
Every time I have
to log in again,

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:19.330
it were a request
for me to touch

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:22.180
the security key, which does
a two-factor authentication.

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:25.030
And you can, of course, have
these saved on computers

00:08:25.030 --> 00:08:27.700
that you trust for 30 days.

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:29.920
That's a choice the user has.

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:33.549
In addition to that, I
showed you, initially,

00:08:33.549 --> 00:08:34.659
the enforcement.

00:08:34.659 --> 00:08:36.990
I showed you the
self-enroll as the user.

00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:40.705
Now I'm going back to the admin
view to deal with a situation

00:08:40.705 --> 00:08:42.970
where the user has
lost the security key.

00:08:42.970 --> 00:08:46.000
So that's the recover
that Reena mentioned.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:48.790
In this case, let's say that
Italo lost his security key.

00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:50.860
They called in,
let the admin know.

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:55.060
The first thing you would
do is go into the user.

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:04.210
And then go into the
security key settings,

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:06.100
and revoke the security key.

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:11.350
That basically makes whatever
was lost completely unusable.

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:13.360
So here is for that
user the security key

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:15.220
that was just registered here.

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:17.410
And then you can revoke it.

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:18.110
Done.

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:20.660
That security key cannot
be used anymore until

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:22.830
it's re-registered
to somewhere else.

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:27.200
The other step is you can
immediately provide that user

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:29.300
with OTP codes.

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:31.880
So that while they are
without a security key,

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:34.820
they can continue to do
their work until they receive

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:37.820
only one, register that
again, and then they

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:39.620
are able to use the
security keys again.

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:42.080
These keys or these
codes will only

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:45.360
work during that grace period
that you had set up before.

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:47.350
So that's how we can
enforce it's still

00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:50.440
two-factor authentication,
even in the case of losing

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:51.340
the security key.

00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:54.380
And in addition to that,
there is the reporting side.

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:57.770
So the Admin Console
has built in reports

00:09:57.770 --> 00:10:00.260
that allow you to track
how many people have

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:04.460
enrolled on security keys,
which users are still pending.

00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:07.220
And that way, you can plan
to extend your security key

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:09.650
enrollment period,
whether you're on track,

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:11.210
and make decision
how to deploy it.

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:14.480
So again, security keys
are very easy for the admin

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:18.500
to set up, very easy for
the users to actually use

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:19.920
on a daily basis.

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:24.750
And they do enforce a very
important part of security,

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:26.540
which is preventing phishing.

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:28.040
And now I'm going
to call Reena back

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:31.640
to talk about that
next step on the issues

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:32.720
that IT worries about.

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:33.637
Thank you.

00:10:33.637 --> 00:10:34.970
REENA NADKARNI: Thank you, Ivon.

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:47.520
Next, we're going to
talk about data breaches.

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:49.560
This is a very important area.

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:54.150
Because data breaches are
growing at an alarming rate.

00:10:54.150 --> 00:10:55.680
I think you're
familiar with this.

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:59.340
But just in the last year,
we've had our hackers

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:05.010
infiltrate some very trusted
names, Sony, Target, Home

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:09.960
Depot, JP Morgan, eBay, Yahoo,
and even the health insurance

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:11.950
provider Anthem.

00:11:11.950 --> 00:11:18.270
We've had over 1,000 data
breaches occur just in 2016.

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:20.940
Over 35 million
critical personnel

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:25.370
records have now been
compromised to hackers.

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:27.810
I'm happy to tell
you that we have

00:11:27.810 --> 00:11:31.230
several new features
in this area to help

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:35.000
protect your data better.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:40.190
As you know, over a year
ago, we launched Gmail DLP.

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:42.290
What this feature
allowed you to do

00:11:42.290 --> 00:11:45.800
was to stop end users
in your organization

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:49.850
from leaking data
accidentally or intentionally.

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:52.250
It allowed you to
either stop people

00:11:52.250 --> 00:11:57.350
from leaking the information,
or be able to send it,

00:11:57.350 --> 00:12:00.170
but give them a warning that
they shouldn't be sending it,

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:04.150
and notify the admin.

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:09.310
We're now extending that DLP
platform to Drive, as well.

00:12:09.310 --> 00:12:12.460
We recently announced this
functionality just last month,

00:12:12.460 --> 00:12:17.920
that DLP is now available
for Drive as well.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:21.690
What we're doing in DLP for
Drive is that, first of all,

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:25.110
we have many predefined
content detectors for you.

00:12:25.110 --> 00:12:29.820
So this is sensitive data
formats from across the world.

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:33.240
This includes license numbers,
social security numbers,

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:35.310
and such from the United States.

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:37.350
You could have CPF
numbers from Brazil--

00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:41.370
so Ivon's confidential
information is not compromised.

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:43.950
We have PAN account
numbers from India.

00:12:43.950 --> 00:12:50.090
And worldwide, we have over
50 predefined content types.

00:12:50.090 --> 00:12:53.450
We also have predefined
content thresholds.

00:12:53.450 --> 00:12:55.940
A lot of the tools on
the market, what they do

00:12:55.940 --> 00:12:58.910
is that they provide you
with so many false positives,

00:12:58.910 --> 00:13:00.710
that as an admin,
you kind of get

00:13:00.710 --> 00:13:02.630
tired of getting these alerts.

00:13:02.630 --> 00:13:04.130
And you may miss something.

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:06.380
So instead, what we
are allowing you to do

00:13:06.380 --> 00:13:13.570
is that you can adjust these to
your level of perceived risk.

00:13:13.570 --> 00:13:17.500
So we talked a little bit about
the content detectors, as well

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:18.890
as the thresholds.

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:20.980
But what's important
here is that we also

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:24.130
provide flexibility
as part of the system.

00:13:24.130 --> 00:13:29.560
So you can not only have these
predefined content detectors,

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:32.170
but let's say your health
care company where you're

00:13:32.170 --> 00:13:35.650
doing research and the keywords
that you want to protect

00:13:35.650 --> 00:13:37.610
haven't been invented yet.

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:40.210
So we allow you to
be able to upload

00:13:40.210 --> 00:13:42.820
a set of keywords that are
relevant to your business

00:13:42.820 --> 00:13:44.900
and your data.

00:13:44.900 --> 00:13:50.200
We also allow you to look at
optical character recognition

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:52.570
on these attachments
that are going up.

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:55.510
So just to be very clear
on what's happening here,

00:13:55.510 --> 00:13:58.780
and the power of our
platform, not only

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:01.970
are we scanning the emails
to protect your data,

00:14:01.970 --> 00:14:04.300
we're scanning the
attachments that are going out

00:14:04.300 --> 00:14:06.370
with these emails.

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:09.580
We can even scan, not only
these emails and attachments,

00:14:09.580 --> 00:14:11.290
but zip attachments.

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:14.170
And on these zip attachments,
if there are images,

00:14:14.170 --> 00:14:16.900
we can do pattern matching,
and do optical character

00:14:16.900 --> 00:14:20.710
recognition for each of these
content types, like credit card

00:14:20.710 --> 00:14:22.810
numbers, and social
security numbers.

00:14:22.810 --> 00:14:25.930
So it's a really
powerful way to stop data

00:14:25.930 --> 00:14:28.000
from leaking outside
your organization.

00:14:31.850 --> 00:14:34.540
So with that, I'm going
to call Ivon to do a demo

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:36.570
and show you how
this actually works.

00:14:36.570 --> 00:14:37.600
Ivon.

00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:40.072
IVON PASSOS: Thanks, Reena.

00:14:40.072 --> 00:14:45.990
OK, So I'm going to, again,
play two roles, admin and user.

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:48.470
And in this case,
the user is Reena,

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:52.740
who is working on a project to
basically review all the credit

00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:54.670
card expenses in the company.

00:14:54.670 --> 00:14:57.210
So this document
was created here

00:14:57.210 --> 00:15:00.820
to start the project
and the collection.

00:15:00.820 --> 00:15:03.686
And it has a lot of
credit card numbers.

00:15:03.686 --> 00:15:05.810
Those numbers, of course,
are sensitive information

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:08.620
that is also information
about employees here.

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:11.310
And you will notice that
the first thing that's

00:15:11.310 --> 00:15:15.210
visible here, on the Share
button, there is a shield.

00:15:15.210 --> 00:15:17.200
That's already drive
the OP, saying,

00:15:17.200 --> 00:15:19.840
if you're going to share
this information, be careful.

00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:23.110
And it even says, this has
credit card number information.

00:15:23.110 --> 00:15:25.380
So be careful when you
share this with anyone.

00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:27.420
And of course, Reena
needs to share this

00:15:27.420 --> 00:15:28.960
with the rest of the team.

00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:31.290
So she's going to
go here and try

00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:35.710
to share this with her
colleague, Freddie Mac.

00:15:35.710 --> 00:15:37.140
Notice what happens here.

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:39.190
Reena probably has
communicated with Freddy

00:15:39.190 --> 00:15:43.200
on both personal
and company email.

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:44.477
And both of them showed up.

00:15:44.477 --> 00:15:45.310
Reena is in a hurry.

00:15:45.310 --> 00:15:48.220
So she clicks on
the Gmail address.

00:15:48.220 --> 00:15:49.480
It just shows Freddie Mac now.

00:15:49.480 --> 00:15:52.450
So she will try to send this.

00:15:52.450 --> 00:15:56.530
And what happens is DLP detects
that that is going outside

00:15:56.530 --> 00:16:00.517
of the domain, tells Reena
that this cannot go outside

00:16:00.517 --> 00:16:01.100
of the domain.

00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:02.470
So I'm blocking this.

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:04.210
And once you click
OK, it actually

00:16:04.210 --> 00:16:06.550
allows you to go back
to the same screen,

00:16:06.550 --> 00:16:09.720
and make the changes
that you would need.

00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:11.370
So how does this magic work?

00:16:11.370 --> 00:16:15.300
As you can see,
for Reena's flow,

00:16:15.300 --> 00:16:16.950
this hasn't
impacted, in any way,

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:19.350
the way she would need to work.

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:23.790
She can still reach out to
her colleagues, get work done.

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:29.270
But on the admin side,
this is done very

00:16:29.270 --> 00:16:32.037
simply by setting up rules.

00:16:32.037 --> 00:16:34.370
So these are the rules that
are currently in the system.

00:16:34.370 --> 00:16:36.024
So I'm now in the admin view.

00:16:36.024 --> 00:16:37.940
And the rules that are
currently in the system

00:16:37.940 --> 00:16:40.940
cover different types of data,
including the credit card

00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:43.040
numbers data, which is
the rule that actually

00:16:43.040 --> 00:16:45.650
caught that specific file.

00:16:45.650 --> 00:16:48.140
I'm going to go through the
process of setting up a rule

00:16:48.140 --> 00:16:50.160
just for you to see
how that would work.

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:52.280
So let's say you need
to set up a new rule.

00:16:52.280 --> 00:16:55.610
There are multiple templates
here that you can use to start.

00:16:55.610 --> 00:16:57.320
I'm going to start
from a blank template.

00:16:57.320 --> 00:16:59.300
But you don't need to
if you know already what

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:02.450
you're looking for, and this
fits one of the preexisting

00:17:02.450 --> 00:17:03.830
templates.

00:17:03.830 --> 00:17:05.359
And to create a
rule, you basically

00:17:05.359 --> 00:17:06.900
need to look at three things.

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:09.440
Number one, what
type of data should

00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:11.690
trigger this specific rule?

00:17:11.690 --> 00:17:14.690
In this case, it's Google Drive.

00:17:14.690 --> 00:17:16.540
You would look at conditions.

00:17:16.540 --> 00:17:19.760
And Reena mentioned
some of this before.

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:24.560
Users will define the scope of
those conditions of the rule,

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:28.490
whether it's the entire company,
whether it's specific OUs,

00:17:28.490 --> 00:17:31.000
or even specific groups.

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:33.640
Once you set that up, you
can define the content.

00:17:33.640 --> 00:17:37.360
And in the content, you may
select predefined templates--

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:39.910
those are the templates that
Reena was talking about.

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:44.410
Even my home country,
Brazil, is covered here.

00:17:44.410 --> 00:17:47.830
I'm going to select social
security just for the demo.

00:17:47.830 --> 00:17:50.200
You can also select
custom word lists

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:54.266
that you can build yourself,
or regular expressions.

00:17:54.266 --> 00:17:56.759
And based on that selection,
you can also select,

00:17:56.759 --> 00:17:58.300
as Reena mentioned,
confidence level,

00:17:58.300 --> 00:18:01.020
so you don't get more
false positives than you're

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:02.770
willing to deal with.

00:18:02.770 --> 00:18:06.010
Once you've done that, the next
step is defining the actions.

00:18:06.010 --> 00:18:08.470
So once the trigger
happens, what actions

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:10.570
do I want to be taken?

00:18:10.570 --> 00:18:13.760
You can do one or
both of these options.

00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:19.410
One is notify the super admins
that this trigger happened,

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:23.790
and/or either block
external access simply,

00:18:23.790 --> 00:18:27.700
or simply warn external
access or external sharing.

00:18:27.700 --> 00:18:29.970
In this case, you may
have cases where you say,

00:18:29.970 --> 00:18:32.430
be careful when you're
sharing financial statements,

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:35.070
because they may
not be public yet.

00:18:35.070 --> 00:18:38.880
But do not share any social
security numbers or credit

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:40.350
cards, for instance.

00:18:40.350 --> 00:18:41.380
And that's it.

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:42.520
It's that simple.

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:45.870
So in order that you
implement DLP in your domain,

00:18:45.870 --> 00:18:48.780
you would basically need
to set up these rules.

00:18:48.780 --> 00:18:52.400
And your users will have no
disruption in the regular flow,

00:18:52.400 --> 00:18:55.635
and still be able to have
the data protected by DLP.

00:18:55.635 --> 00:18:56.610
And that's it.

00:18:56.610 --> 00:18:57.646
Back to you, Reena.

00:18:57.646 --> 00:18:59.520
REENA NADKARNI: Next,
we continue our journey

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:03.090
to put you in better
control of your information.

00:19:03.090 --> 00:19:06.930
The Gmail logs in BigQuery,
what we're doing here

00:19:06.930 --> 00:19:11.280
is allowing you to export all of
your Gmail logs into BigQuery.

00:19:11.280 --> 00:19:13.110
As good as we have
our reporting, we

00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:16.950
have customers telling us, hey,
can I do my own custom queries?

00:19:16.950 --> 00:19:18.900
So this will allow
you to do that.

00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:21.330
This allows you to
retain your data

00:19:21.330 --> 00:19:24.060
for longer periods
of time, as well as,

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:26.700
you can use a data
visualization tool

00:19:26.700 --> 00:19:29.310
like Data Studio from Google.

00:19:29.310 --> 00:19:32.550
Or you could use third-party
tools, like Tableau or Splunk,

00:19:32.550 --> 00:19:37.350
that you're already using
within your environment.

00:19:37.350 --> 00:19:41.370
Staying with Gmail, S/MIME
my encryption for Gmail--

00:19:41.370 --> 00:19:43.770
as you know, S/MIME is
an industry standard way

00:19:43.770 --> 00:19:45.710
of encrypting email.

00:19:45.710 --> 00:19:49.470
What's special here is that
we are allowing our customers

00:19:49.470 --> 00:19:52.920
to bring their own certificates
to be able to do encryption

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:55.260
using their own certificates.

00:19:55.260 --> 00:19:57.780
We're also providing
an admin API

00:19:57.780 --> 00:20:01.080
to be able to do this at
scale for large companies.

00:20:03.950 --> 00:20:06.230
We've talked a lot
about the new features

00:20:06.230 --> 00:20:08.210
that are coming up with Gmail.

00:20:08.210 --> 00:20:10.040
However, I want to
point out that there

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:12.920
have been many features
of Gmail security

00:20:12.920 --> 00:20:15.740
that have been existing
in place for a long time

00:20:15.740 --> 00:20:18.680
that people don't
realize we already have.

00:20:18.680 --> 00:20:20.970
One example that
is not listed here,

00:20:20.970 --> 00:20:24.080
but the TLS warning
that we recently

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:27.140
added, the Transport Layer
Security Warning, that

00:20:27.140 --> 00:20:30.230
just gave an indication
if a domain did not

00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:31.820
encrypt their email.

00:20:31.820 --> 00:20:35.710
And that fundamentally changed
how many domains out there

00:20:35.710 --> 00:20:37.550
were doing TLS.

00:20:37.550 --> 00:20:42.350
The next one here is to prevent
outgoing spam with DMARC.

00:20:42.350 --> 00:20:46.490
This allowed people to
control someone else

00:20:46.490 --> 00:20:50.090
from going out there and
sending unauthenticated emails

00:20:50.090 --> 00:20:51.710
on your behalf.

00:20:51.710 --> 00:20:55.550
And then finally, we also have
really solid spam and abuse

00:20:55.550 --> 00:21:00.770
policies in place where Gmail
is doing the work for you.

00:21:00.770 --> 00:21:02.420
Along with all of
these features,

00:21:02.420 --> 00:21:06.410
we also have security workshops
that we run for our customers.

00:21:06.410 --> 00:21:08.990
So about three months
after the product G Suite

00:21:08.990 --> 00:21:11.240
gets deployed in
your environment,

00:21:11.240 --> 00:21:14.420
either someone from Google
or someone from a partner

00:21:14.420 --> 00:21:17.150
can come and run a
security workshop for you.

00:21:17.150 --> 00:21:20.330
And they can come and verify
all of your security settings

00:21:20.330 --> 00:21:22.880
to make sure that you are
appropriately protected.

00:21:26.030 --> 00:21:28.760
Deep scanning of
Gmail attachments,

00:21:28.760 --> 00:21:31.970
this is one of my favorite
upcoming features.

00:21:31.970 --> 00:21:36.410
Its already available in trusted
tester for our customers.

00:21:36.410 --> 00:21:41.180
The time between a first
malicious email entering

00:21:41.180 --> 00:21:45.140
into your organization,
to the first user opening

00:21:45.140 --> 00:21:47.780
this attachment, and
then spreading malware

00:21:47.780 --> 00:21:51.650
into your entire
organization, is very little.

00:21:51.650 --> 00:21:55.490
So what you want to do as an
IT administrator is ideally

00:21:55.490 --> 00:21:57.410
get ahead of that process.

00:21:57.410 --> 00:22:00.320
So with the pre-delivery
scanning of email attachments,

00:22:00.320 --> 00:22:04.490
what we're able to do is we're
able to open these attachments

00:22:04.490 --> 00:22:07.910
in a secure sandbox
environment, run

00:22:07.910 --> 00:22:11.720
them to make sure that there
are no malicious scripts

00:22:11.720 --> 00:22:15.200
or bad acting going on in
each of these attachments.

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:17.060
So this is something
to watch out for.

00:22:17.060 --> 00:22:21.790
And it's already available
in trusted tester.

00:22:21.790 --> 00:22:24.340
Switching gears a little
bit to a different product

00:22:24.340 --> 00:22:27.000
in our portfolio is Google+.

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:30.370
As you know, Google+ is a
relatively new addition.

00:22:30.370 --> 00:22:34.360
I think in Q3 of last year we
added Google+ to our commercial

00:22:34.360 --> 00:22:35.500
suite.

00:22:35.500 --> 00:22:38.620
A lot of large customers
are using this product

00:22:38.620 --> 00:22:42.400
to make cultural change happen
within their organization.

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:44.290
I'll give you some examples.

00:22:44.290 --> 00:22:47.380
Rolling out large initiatives
from their executives--

00:22:47.380 --> 00:22:49.630
for some reason there are
always those three-pronged

00:22:49.630 --> 00:22:50.980
initiatives, right?

00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:53.290
So to be able to roll
out these initiatives

00:22:53.290 --> 00:22:56.320
in your organization
across multiple geos,

00:22:56.320 --> 00:22:57.820
our customers are using Google+.

00:22:57.820 --> 00:23:02.080
They're also using Google+
to do bottoms-up ideation.

00:23:02.080 --> 00:23:04.390
We have a large
retail customer where

00:23:04.390 --> 00:23:07.630
they have store employees
who have really good ideas

00:23:07.630 --> 00:23:10.630
to be able to improve
the overall company.

00:23:10.630 --> 00:23:12.130
And until now,
they were just not

00:23:12.130 --> 00:23:13.990
able to get that voice heard.

00:23:13.990 --> 00:23:18.250
So they are using these dynamic
Communities across the company

00:23:18.250 --> 00:23:20.230
to bring out these ideas.

00:23:20.230 --> 00:23:24.400
So I'm happy to share that
we have several new admin

00:23:24.400 --> 00:23:27.790
features that are now
coming up for this product.

00:23:27.790 --> 00:23:29.480
I'll give you some examples.

00:23:29.480 --> 00:23:32.230
The first one is the
domain-level adoption

00:23:32.230 --> 00:23:33.670
engagement metrics.

00:23:33.670 --> 00:23:37.030
What we often hear from admins
is that, hey, this product

00:23:37.030 --> 00:23:37.570
is great.

00:23:37.570 --> 00:23:39.940
It's being used quite
a bit in our company.

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:42.760
But I have no way
to show the impact.

00:23:42.760 --> 00:23:45.100
So now we can show
them how many people

00:23:45.100 --> 00:23:48.190
engaged with certain posts,
who plussed one certain posts,

00:23:48.190 --> 00:23:49.420
and such.

00:23:49.420 --> 00:23:52.030
Second, we have
the audit log API.

00:23:52.030 --> 00:23:53.830
What this does, we
have one customer--

00:23:53.830 --> 00:23:55.030
I'll give you an example.

00:23:55.030 --> 00:23:57.460
We have one customer,
which is a large bank.

00:23:57.460 --> 00:24:00.610
And they have a small group
of users, they're brokers.

00:24:00.610 --> 00:24:04.190
These brokers are required
to be highly monitored.

00:24:04.190 --> 00:24:06.160
And so what they're
doing here is that they

00:24:06.160 --> 00:24:07.690
have brought in a partner.

00:24:07.690 --> 00:24:10.660
That partner is building
a compliance solution

00:24:10.660 --> 00:24:13.900
on top of our audit log APIs.

00:24:13.900 --> 00:24:16.360
Finally, the
number-one requested

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:20.630
feature in this product is
the walled garden feature.

00:24:20.630 --> 00:24:23.080
This allows the
admin to stop people

00:24:23.080 --> 00:24:26.620
from externally sharing
content within their domain

00:24:26.620 --> 00:24:30.520
from outside their domain,
and stopping IP leakage.

00:24:30.520 --> 00:24:33.790
So that full-domain sharing
and such restrictions

00:24:33.790 --> 00:24:36.130
is what we internally
called as walled garden.

00:24:36.130 --> 00:24:40.420
And that's coming soon, as well.

00:24:40.420 --> 00:24:44.250
So we talked a lot about
these solid protections

00:24:44.250 --> 00:24:47.190
that we're putting in place
for our first-party products,

00:24:47.190 --> 00:24:51.430
like email, for
Drive, and others.

00:24:51.430 --> 00:24:54.310
But what about
third-party applications?

00:24:58.530 --> 00:25:04.220
We have a new feature in place,
third-party apps white listing.

00:25:04.220 --> 00:25:08.960
And this is also already
available in trusted tester.

00:25:08.960 --> 00:25:12.440
What this does is that it
allows you to figure out

00:25:12.440 --> 00:25:16.700
which applications are getting
access to which OAuth scopes.

00:25:16.700 --> 00:25:19.190
And then the admin
can only offer

00:25:19.190 --> 00:25:22.010
a set of trusted
applications the ability

00:25:22.010 --> 00:25:23.990
to get to company data.

00:25:23.990 --> 00:25:27.290
A good example of this would
be the Concur Travel app.

00:25:27.290 --> 00:25:28.160
You trust it.

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:32.270
It can have access to all of
your drive data, for example.

00:25:32.270 --> 00:25:35.300
But there are many apps
that are bad actors.

00:25:35.300 --> 00:25:37.910
And they overreach, in
terms of the OAuth scopes

00:25:37.910 --> 00:25:39.860
that they ask for.

00:25:39.860 --> 00:25:43.010
Recently, there was
a customer where

00:25:43.010 --> 00:25:44.990
there was a very
innocent, innocuous

00:25:44.990 --> 00:25:46.760
looking clock application.

00:25:46.760 --> 00:25:49.010
And many of their users
installed this clock

00:25:49.010 --> 00:25:50.030
application.

00:25:50.030 --> 00:25:52.970
And it got access to a
lot of the company data.

00:25:52.970 --> 00:25:55.820
If someone, some app like
this, a malicious app,

00:25:55.820 --> 00:25:58.490
got access to all of
your users' files,

00:25:58.490 --> 00:26:01.130
just imagine the kind
of damage it could do.

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:06.670
So I'm going to
call Ivon on stage

00:26:06.670 --> 00:26:09.930
to show us a demo of
third-party apps white listing.

00:26:09.930 --> 00:26:12.470
IVON PASSOS: Thank you, Reena.

00:26:12.470 --> 00:26:16.520
All right, so here
we see, again,

00:26:16.520 --> 00:26:20.180
Reena, who is trying
to be all she can be,

00:26:20.180 --> 00:26:22.940
looking for apps to
increase her productivity.

00:26:22.940 --> 00:26:26.330
And there are two specific
apps that she found.

00:26:26.330 --> 00:26:30.930
One is DocHub, which allows
you to edit, sign PDFs

00:26:30.930 --> 00:26:33.860
online integrated with G Suite.

00:26:33.860 --> 00:26:35.510
The other one is
Smartsheet, which

00:26:35.510 --> 00:26:38.750
allows you to turn your
sheets in your system

00:26:38.750 --> 00:26:41.690
into a project
management environment.

00:26:41.690 --> 00:26:42.990
So Reena said, OK, great.

00:26:42.990 --> 00:26:45.320
I'm going to try
to use these tools.

00:26:45.320 --> 00:26:50.160
And as she tried to log
in, Smartsheet worked.

00:26:50.160 --> 00:26:53.130
And now she try to
log in to DocHub.

00:26:53.130 --> 00:26:56.280
She got this message,
policy enforced.

00:26:56.280 --> 00:26:59.020
And she has no
idea what that is.

00:26:59.020 --> 00:27:02.650
So she gets in touch
with the admin.

00:27:02.650 --> 00:27:05.830
And the admin actually
knows what's going on.

00:27:05.830 --> 00:27:09.640
What the admin did first
is, because of those threats

00:27:09.640 --> 00:27:14.200
that Reena mentioned in the
[INAUDIBLE] not the employee,

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:19.000
they had blocked any access
from any third-party app

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:21.064
to the G Suite API.

00:27:21.064 --> 00:27:22.480
And basically,
what we are looking

00:27:22.480 --> 00:27:25.750
at here is you have granular
controls where you can say,

00:27:25.750 --> 00:27:30.810
for these specific apps,
I want to block everyone.

00:27:30.810 --> 00:27:33.280
For these other ones, I
want to leave it open.

00:27:33.280 --> 00:27:36.370
In this case, we had
blocked Drive and Contacts,

00:27:36.370 --> 00:27:38.710
left Gmail and Calendar open.

00:27:38.710 --> 00:27:40.930
And that basically says
that any third party

00:27:40.930 --> 00:27:46.020
app who try to access the
G Suite API will fail.

00:27:46.020 --> 00:27:49.140
You can also allow
your own domain apps

00:27:49.140 --> 00:27:51.210
to be automatically trusted.

00:27:51.210 --> 00:27:53.245
However, you'll notice
that one app worked.

00:27:53.245 --> 00:27:54.120
The other one didn't.

00:27:54.120 --> 00:27:55.380
Why?

00:27:55.380 --> 00:27:58.650
As Reena mentioned with the
third-party app white listing,

00:27:58.650 --> 00:28:03.680
you are actually able
to individually list

00:28:03.680 --> 00:28:05.240
the apps that you trust.

00:28:05.240 --> 00:28:08.300
In this case,
Smartsheets is listed.

00:28:08.300 --> 00:28:10.700
And everybody else gets blocked.

00:28:10.700 --> 00:28:13.430
In this case, that's
why DocHub was blocked.

00:28:13.430 --> 00:28:16.940
Now DocHub is actually a
G Suite marketplace app.

00:28:16.940 --> 00:28:18.650
So as an admin, I
can make the decision

00:28:18.650 --> 00:28:20.900
to say, well, I know
we can trust this app.

00:28:20.900 --> 00:28:22.940
This is not an app
coming from nowhere.

00:28:22.940 --> 00:28:24.950
It's been certified by Google.

00:28:24.950 --> 00:28:28.060
I can just go here.

00:28:28.060 --> 00:28:29.280
DocHub is right there.

00:28:29.280 --> 00:28:31.980
I can add it to the white list.

00:28:31.980 --> 00:28:35.760
And it's that easy
for you to increase

00:28:35.760 --> 00:28:38.580
the number of apps that are
supported that you trust,

00:28:38.580 --> 00:28:41.580
so that you reach a
balance between protecting

00:28:41.580 --> 00:28:45.310
your domain and your data
and enabling your employees'

00:28:45.310 --> 00:28:46.750
productivity.

00:28:46.750 --> 00:28:50.040
So that's basically
it, that's simple.

00:28:50.040 --> 00:28:52.330
Thanks, Reena.

00:28:52.330 --> 00:28:53.830
REENA NADKARNI: So
next, we're going

00:28:53.830 --> 00:29:03.380
to talk about Vault. Vault is
our eDiscovery product that

00:29:03.380 --> 00:29:05.700
comes as part of--
wait a minute.

00:29:05.700 --> 00:29:08.727
Ivon is the actual product
manager for Vault. Ivon,

00:29:08.727 --> 00:29:11.060
you want to come up here and
tell us about your product?

00:29:11.060 --> 00:29:14.470
He's been using me as a bad
actor in all of his demos.

00:29:14.470 --> 00:29:16.490
So I'm going to put
him on the spot.

00:29:16.490 --> 00:29:19.040
Come on up here, Ivon, and
talk about your product.

00:29:19.040 --> 00:29:21.090
IVON PASSOS: Thank
you very much, Reena.

00:29:21.090 --> 00:29:23.330
I'll see what I can do.

00:29:23.330 --> 00:29:25.520
All right-- and by the
way, Reena is my manager.

00:29:25.520 --> 00:29:28.550
So I better do a good job here.

00:29:28.550 --> 00:29:31.880
So Google Vault is the
eDiscovery and compliance

00:29:31.880 --> 00:29:34.350
solution for G Suite.

00:29:34.350 --> 00:29:35.700
How does Vault do that?

00:29:35.700 --> 00:29:40.110
So Vault basically provides
you two specific areas

00:29:40.110 --> 00:29:43.860
of functionality to allow
you to have legal compliance

00:29:43.860 --> 00:29:45.150
and prevent data loss.

00:29:45.150 --> 00:29:48.360
Number one, through
its retention policies,

00:29:48.360 --> 00:29:51.780
Vault allows you to
control the specific data

00:29:51.780 --> 00:29:54.540
that you should keep in your
environment, and the data

00:29:54.540 --> 00:29:56.430
that you don't want to keep.

00:29:56.430 --> 00:29:59.280
And that allows
you to be compliant

00:29:59.280 --> 00:30:04.470
with your requirements, as well
as minimize your data liability

00:30:04.470 --> 00:30:05.160
risk.

00:30:05.160 --> 00:30:07.950
If you leave data that's too
old, when you get to the time

00:30:07.950 --> 00:30:09.780
where you need to
produce data, that data

00:30:09.780 --> 00:30:11.490
may not be required to be there.

00:30:11.490 --> 00:30:13.860
But it may actually
create trouble for you.

00:30:13.860 --> 00:30:17.780
And yes, too much data, in
some cases, is a problem.

00:30:17.780 --> 00:30:19.750
Once you've done that
in your environment,

00:30:19.750 --> 00:30:21.530
and you've set those
automatic policies

00:30:21.530 --> 00:30:24.440
to keep your environment
always compliant,

00:30:24.440 --> 00:30:27.950
Vault also provides you
with eDiscovery tools

00:30:27.950 --> 00:30:30.094
that allow you support
both legal and compliance

00:30:30.094 --> 00:30:31.760
requirements, whether
they are certainly

00:30:31.760 --> 00:30:37.490
investigations, or specific
legal cases and lawsuits.

00:30:37.490 --> 00:30:39.950
You can search and
produce data, and get

00:30:39.950 --> 00:30:41.990
that in a format
that can be used

00:30:41.990 --> 00:30:44.570
for sharing within
external counsel

00:30:44.570 --> 00:30:47.360
or with your internal teams,
as well as moving that

00:30:47.360 --> 00:30:51.590
into a specific eDiscovery
tool that follows up

00:30:51.590 --> 00:30:53.850
with the process.

00:30:53.850 --> 00:30:56.670
So here are some details
on how this works.

00:30:56.670 --> 00:30:59.310
With retention
policies, you basically

00:30:59.310 --> 00:31:02.740
can answer a few questions.

00:31:02.740 --> 00:31:05.387
First question is, what
app do you want to cover?

00:31:05.387 --> 00:31:07.470
And one thing that we would
like to mention, which

00:31:07.470 --> 00:31:11.080
was announced this
morning, is that now we

00:31:11.080 --> 00:31:12.480
have full support for Gmail--

00:31:12.480 --> 00:31:16.560
which we had-- and we've added
new full support for Drive,

00:31:16.560 --> 00:31:19.327
including Team Drives,
as well as Google Groups.

00:31:19.327 --> 00:31:20.910
So basically, you
can make a selection

00:31:20.910 --> 00:31:22.830
of the app you need cover.

00:31:22.830 --> 00:31:25.120
The next step is to
define the scope.

00:31:25.120 --> 00:31:27.270
Notice, there is a
similarity with other things

00:31:27.270 --> 00:31:30.120
we do, like DLP, where you
go with specific criteria

00:31:30.120 --> 00:31:31.420
that you're going to use.

00:31:31.420 --> 00:31:33.870
So here you can do
organizational unit,

00:31:33.870 --> 00:31:35.410
entire domain.

00:31:35.410 --> 00:31:39.450
You can also do specific
entities like Team Drives.

00:31:39.450 --> 00:31:41.290
The next step is duration.

00:31:41.290 --> 00:31:44.770
Do I want this policy to be
applicable indefinitely, just

00:31:44.770 --> 00:31:49.370
keep all the data, or set it
to a specific number of days?

00:31:49.370 --> 00:31:51.100
And last but not
least, what should

00:31:51.100 --> 00:31:53.500
I do once that policy expire?

00:31:53.500 --> 00:31:56.050
Should I get rid
of any data that

00:31:56.050 --> 00:31:58.821
matches it, regardless of
whether the user has deleted it

00:31:58.821 --> 00:31:59.320
or not?

00:31:59.320 --> 00:32:01.080
That's a very aggressive policy.

00:32:01.080 --> 00:32:04.810
Or should I only get rid of
data that the user has already

00:32:04.810 --> 00:32:05.960
deleted?

00:32:05.960 --> 00:32:09.160
Again, a lot of flexibility,
whether it's choosing a simple

00:32:09.160 --> 00:32:09.810
UI.

00:32:09.810 --> 00:32:11.800
And you can have
multiple policies

00:32:11.800 --> 00:32:16.090
that, together, will
match your compliance

00:32:16.090 --> 00:32:17.980
needs for your company.

00:32:17.980 --> 00:32:22.000
Once that's set, you would
get Google-powered search

00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:26.750
to get the data that you
need for a specific project.

00:32:26.750 --> 00:32:29.579
And once you get to the
level of data that you want--

00:32:29.579 --> 00:32:31.870
you've culled the data,
narrowed it down to your search

00:32:31.870 --> 00:32:33.040
requirements--

00:32:33.040 --> 00:32:36.520
you can export that easily,
so that you can share that

00:32:36.520 --> 00:32:38.480
with whoever needs it.

00:32:38.480 --> 00:32:39.590
And that's it.

00:32:39.590 --> 00:32:42.290
This is Google Vault, quick
and easy legal discovery

00:32:42.290 --> 00:32:43.730
and compliance.

00:32:43.730 --> 00:32:44.757
How did I do?

00:32:44.757 --> 00:32:48.356
REENA NADKARNI: [LAUGHING]
What do you guys think?

00:32:48.356 --> 00:32:49.340
[APPLAUSE]

00:32:49.340 --> 00:32:50.298
IVON PASSOS: Thank you.

00:32:50.298 --> 00:32:52.790
REENA NADKARNI: You know,
that was great, Ivon.

00:32:52.790 --> 00:32:57.010
But to me, it seems like he's
been practicing in his sleep.

00:32:57.010 --> 00:32:58.480
Thank you, Ivon.

00:32:58.480 --> 00:33:00.400
He's the actual product
manager responsible

00:33:00.400 --> 00:33:02.060
for the announcements
that Prabhakar

00:33:02.060 --> 00:33:05.080
made this morning
on Vault. Let's

00:33:05.080 --> 00:33:06.650
talk about Mobile Management.

00:33:09.650 --> 00:33:12.350
Mobile Management,
why is this important?

00:33:12.350 --> 00:33:15.980
There's two fundamental things
going on in Mobile Management.

00:33:15.980 --> 00:33:20.210
We have employees who expect
to be productive on the go

00:33:20.210 --> 00:33:21.540
wherever they are.

00:33:21.540 --> 00:33:24.170
And they don't want
security to get in the way.

00:33:24.170 --> 00:33:26.540
And then you have the
admins, whose goal

00:33:26.540 --> 00:33:30.050
is to keep the
company's data secure.

00:33:30.050 --> 00:33:33.200
And they really, their
whole exposure to risk

00:33:33.200 --> 00:33:35.900
went up multiple times,
because each employee

00:33:35.900 --> 00:33:38.360
now has multiple devices.

00:33:38.360 --> 00:33:41.390
The other problem we have
is that these admins are now

00:33:41.390 --> 00:33:45.080
managing a fundamentally
different workforce.

00:33:45.080 --> 00:33:48.200
People don't stay in the
same job for 20 years.

00:33:48.200 --> 00:33:50.732
We have millennials moving
jobs every few years.

00:33:50.732 --> 00:33:52.190
And then when they
do that, you got

00:33:52.190 --> 00:33:55.100
to revoke the access they
have on their devices,

00:33:55.100 --> 00:33:56.660
or take that
company-owned device

00:33:56.660 --> 00:33:59.390
and redeploy it
to somebody else.

00:33:59.390 --> 00:34:02.060
We have people trying to
jailbreak their iPhones.

00:34:02.060 --> 00:34:04.430
We have people trying to
side load applications

00:34:04.430 --> 00:34:07.850
that they're not authorized
to on their company devices.

00:34:07.850 --> 00:34:09.409
We need to be able
to give admins

00:34:09.409 --> 00:34:12.590
the tools to be able to
control this if they need to.

00:34:16.590 --> 00:34:18.570
What many customers
don't realize

00:34:18.570 --> 00:34:22.050
is that, as part
of the G Suite, we

00:34:22.050 --> 00:34:25.830
have a comprehensive mobile
device management solution.

00:34:25.830 --> 00:34:29.611
I'm going to walk you through
a quick set of features

00:34:29.611 --> 00:34:30.819
that come with this solution.

00:34:34.770 --> 00:34:38.780
So first, right from
our one console,

00:34:38.780 --> 00:34:41.150
you're able to manage
both the company owned,

00:34:41.150 --> 00:34:43.310
as well as bring
your own devices.

00:34:43.310 --> 00:34:47.804
You're able to curate and
deploy third-party applications.

00:34:47.804 --> 00:34:49.429
You can look at some
reporting that I'm

00:34:49.429 --> 00:34:51.250
going to show you
here pretty quickly,

00:34:51.250 --> 00:34:54.980
as well as take some actions
using our mobile device

00:34:54.980 --> 00:34:56.840
management solution.

00:34:56.840 --> 00:35:00.410
So here is an example of a
very simple one click set up.

00:35:00.410 --> 00:35:02.180
You click on Manage Now.

00:35:02.180 --> 00:35:03.680
And it tells you
it's going to allow

00:35:03.680 --> 00:35:06.050
you to do screen
lock account wipe,

00:35:06.050 --> 00:35:08.150
as well as inventory management.

00:35:08.150 --> 00:35:09.530
You click on Manage Now.

00:35:16.350 --> 00:35:17.050
And there.

00:35:17.050 --> 00:35:20.220
Now mobile device management
is set up as complete.

00:35:20.220 --> 00:35:26.360
And you are able to set this up
for your entire mobile fleet.

00:35:26.360 --> 00:35:29.930
Next, bulk enrollment
of company devices--

00:35:29.930 --> 00:35:31.290
here's what happens.

00:35:31.290 --> 00:35:33.800
Let's say you're an IT
admin managing a fleet

00:35:33.800 --> 00:35:36.230
of about 20,000 employees.

00:35:36.230 --> 00:35:39.110
And now your CEO just
complicated your life

00:35:39.110 --> 00:35:43.260
by acquiring another company
with 3,000 new users.

00:35:43.260 --> 00:35:45.170
Now you bought these
new devices to be

00:35:45.170 --> 00:35:48.200
able to get these
users to be productive.

00:35:48.200 --> 00:35:49.460
What do you do?

00:35:49.460 --> 00:35:52.670
We provide a way for you to just
take all of these device IDs,

00:35:52.670 --> 00:35:55.610
put them in a CSV
file, and upload them.

00:35:55.610 --> 00:35:57.590
And our system can ingest them.

00:35:57.590 --> 00:35:59.780
And with that simple
thing, you can

00:35:59.780 --> 00:36:02.660
get all of these devices
managed pretty quickly.

00:36:02.660 --> 00:36:03.950
That's MDM at scale.

00:36:06.610 --> 00:36:11.320
You also may have some
homegrown custom applications,

00:36:11.320 --> 00:36:14.230
or you may want to deploy
some pre-approved third-party

00:36:14.230 --> 00:36:15.500
applications.

00:36:15.500 --> 00:36:17.845
We allow you to do that
using our work store.

00:36:21.010 --> 00:36:24.370
You're also able to monitor
and audit these devices.

00:36:24.370 --> 00:36:28.410
You can report on the type of
users, the type of devices, iOS

00:36:28.410 --> 00:36:30.020
and Android versions, and such.

00:36:35.420 --> 00:36:39.020
What we've also found is
that, in many large customers,

00:36:39.020 --> 00:36:40.630
there's not just one admin.

00:36:40.630 --> 00:36:42.050
There's teams of admins.

00:36:42.050 --> 00:36:43.670
Some of these admins
have delegated

00:36:43.670 --> 00:36:47.520
admin authority managing
just Gmail or just mobile.

00:36:47.520 --> 00:36:50.150
So we want to be able
to enable that scenario.

00:36:50.150 --> 00:36:52.130
And you can have a
comprehensive audit

00:36:52.130 --> 00:36:56.350
log of what admin actions were
taken by each of these admins.

00:36:59.740 --> 00:37:02.830
Next, we also have
a risk when there

00:37:02.830 --> 00:37:06.370
is inactive devices with the
credentials of your company's

00:37:06.370 --> 00:37:09.100
data sitting on these
inactive devices.

00:37:09.100 --> 00:37:10.930
So what we're
enabling you to do is

00:37:10.930 --> 00:37:13.690
to be able to track
these inactive devices.

00:37:13.690 --> 00:37:15.610
And you can revoke
credentials if you

00:37:15.610 --> 00:37:16.930
don't want them to be there.

00:37:20.140 --> 00:37:22.290
So once you have done this
monitoring and auditing,

00:37:22.290 --> 00:37:24.930
you can also take some
very quick actions.

00:37:24.930 --> 00:37:28.470
You can, for example, revoke
the Google OAuth Token.

00:37:28.470 --> 00:37:32.439
Or you can also remove the
Google Account all together.

00:37:36.570 --> 00:37:38.800
At Google, especially
in our team,

00:37:38.800 --> 00:37:41.590
because we are focused on
large customers and admins,

00:37:41.590 --> 00:37:45.460
we like to remind people
that admins are people too.

00:37:45.460 --> 00:37:48.730
So as an admin, you may
possibly be somewhere,

00:37:48.730 --> 00:37:52.210
like at your child's birthday
party, and you get an alert.

00:37:52.210 --> 00:37:55.240
So we enable you to
take these actions even

00:37:55.240 --> 00:37:59.188
on the go using our Google
Mobile Admin application.

00:38:03.180 --> 00:38:06.840
We also allow you to
create custom workflows

00:38:06.840 --> 00:38:10.380
for these devices
using our admin APIs.

00:38:10.380 --> 00:38:13.620
So for example, we have
our mobile cloud APIs.

00:38:13.620 --> 00:38:16.740
We have mobile audit
APIs that you can use.

00:38:16.740 --> 00:38:19.410
And it's also a way
for our partners

00:38:19.410 --> 00:38:22.380
to build and extend
our platforms

00:38:22.380 --> 00:38:24.270
using these mobile APIs.

00:38:28.900 --> 00:38:32.140
As customers are discovering
these features and capabilities

00:38:32.140 --> 00:38:34.660
of the Google mobile device
management, what they're

00:38:34.660 --> 00:38:36.280
realizing is that
they are paying

00:38:36.280 --> 00:38:39.130
extra for a niche product
that they don't need to.

00:38:39.130 --> 00:38:42.610
Because all they were using is
those 37 active sync policies

00:38:42.610 --> 00:38:43.420
anyway.

00:38:43.420 --> 00:38:45.730
So we've seen a
very large uptick

00:38:45.730 --> 00:38:49.000
in the adoption of our mobile
device management solution

00:38:49.000 --> 00:38:51.010
over the last few quarters.

00:38:56.220 --> 00:39:00.660
So ultimately, in the cloud,
security is about trust.

00:39:06.780 --> 00:39:08.880
And as you saw this
morning in the keynote,

00:39:08.880 --> 00:39:12.780
you had Phil Garland from
PWC come and talk to you

00:39:12.780 --> 00:39:15.690
about how they use
G Suite at scale.

00:39:15.690 --> 00:39:18.990
These trust, security,
and compliance features

00:39:18.990 --> 00:39:22.020
have had many large
customers experience

00:39:22.020 --> 00:39:25.620
the power of Google innovation
and machine learning

00:39:25.620 --> 00:39:27.000
by going Google.

00:39:27.000 --> 00:39:29.910
Here's a quote from
Mike, who is the CIO

00:39:29.910 --> 00:39:31.597
of Whirlpool Corporation.

00:39:36.470 --> 00:39:38.060
So let's think about this.

00:39:38.060 --> 00:39:40.050
We encrypt your data at rest.

00:39:40.050 --> 00:39:42.890
We encrypt your data in transit.

00:39:42.890 --> 00:39:46.580
You can use security keys
to secure your accounts.

00:39:46.580 --> 00:39:50.780
In addition to that, our data
centers, our applications,

00:39:50.780 --> 00:39:55.010
and our processes are audited
and independently verified

00:39:55.010 --> 00:39:56.510
by third parties.

00:39:56.510 --> 00:39:58.640
And so we have these
certifications,

00:39:58.640 --> 00:40:05.900
like FedRAMP, ISO
27001, and others.

00:40:05.900 --> 00:40:08.270
One question that
I keep getting--

00:40:08.270 --> 00:40:10.609
and I almost want to
make a sign that says,

00:40:10.609 --> 00:40:12.650
I can't believe I have to
still keep saying this.

00:40:12.650 --> 00:40:14.470
So I'm going to say it.

00:40:14.470 --> 00:40:15.800
But what about ads?

00:40:15.800 --> 00:40:19.010
People ask me, Google is
an advertising company,

00:40:19.010 --> 00:40:20.690
are you going to show me ads?

00:40:20.690 --> 00:40:21.950
So let's be clear.

00:40:21.950 --> 00:40:24.830
In our consumer
products, we have ads.

00:40:24.830 --> 00:40:28.550
And that enables us to offer
these products to our consumers

00:40:28.550 --> 00:40:29.730
for free.

00:40:29.730 --> 00:40:34.340
The enterprise business, the
ads and our enterprise systems,

00:40:34.340 --> 00:40:35.600
they don't touch each other.

00:40:35.600 --> 00:40:40.130
We commit to no ads using
our contractual DPA, the Data

00:40:40.130 --> 00:40:41.285
Protection Addendum.

00:40:43.800 --> 00:40:44.700
So let's recap.

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:46.740
We went through a
lot of things today.

00:40:46.740 --> 00:40:49.650
So let's recap about what
the things we talked about.

